as \\\". So we replace each \" inside the code with \\\".\n\nSo the final JSON string will have many \\n and \\\". I'll write it out.\n\nLet me construct the JSON object manually.\n\nImplementation value string (with escapes):\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    min_sum = sum(s['minimum'] for s in segments)\\n    cap_sum = sum(s['capacity'] for s in segments)\\n    if not (min_sum <= budget <= cap_sum):\\n        return {\\\"error\\\": f\\\"budget {budget} not between min {min_sum} and cap {cap_sum}\\\"}\\n    allocations = {}\\n    for s in segments:\\n        allocations[s['name']] = s['minimum']\\n    remaining = budget - min_sum\\n    seg_list = [(idx, s['name'], s['importance']) for idx, s in enumerate(segments)]\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_score = -1.0\\n        best_seg_name = None\\n        for idx, name, imp in seg_list:\\n            cur = allocations[name]\\n            if cur >= next(s['capacity'] for s in segments if s['name'] == name):\\n                continue\\n            score = imp / (cur + 1)\\n            if score > best_score or (score == best_score and idx < best_idx):\\n                best_score = score\\n                best_idx = idx\\n                best_seg_name = name\\n        if best_seg_name is None:\\n            break\\n        allocations[best_seg_name] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    total = sum(allocations.values())\\n    return {\\n        \\\"allocations\\\": allocations,\\n        \\\"total_allocated\\\": total,\\n        \\\"segment_order\\\": [s['name'] for s in segments]\\n    }\"\n\nBut note that within the code, there are single quotes for dictionary keys like 'segments', 'name', etc. That's fine; no need to escape single quotes in JSON. Also there are curly braces for f-string and dict literals; those are fine.\n\nNow, the entire JSON object must be exactly one valid JSON object. So I'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"d